  "summary": "# Daily Health-Tech Digest — August 5, 2026\n\nHealthcare is rapidly adopting robotic and physical AI systems, with GE HealthCare launching an AI breast ultrasound system and companies testing in-home robots that reveal unexpected clinical trends, signaling a potential shift toward more automated care delivery. Artificial intelligence is also reshaping drug development and clinical operations—Pathos acquired two cancer drugs for AI-driven development, and Novo Nordisk partnered with H1 on AI for discovery and trials—though questions persist about regulatory classification, with UK regulators now weighing whether AI scribes qualify as medical devices. The sector faces mounting cybersecurity challenges as AI-driven attacks require new defensive strategies beyond traditional approaches, exemplified by the AnMed cyberattack payment scam and broader governance gaps in AI tool deployment. On the policy front, Medicaid coverage disputes have intensified with state directors contradicting administration claims about cuts, while orphan drug exemptions face scrutiny over Medicare negotiation carve-outs, and rural areas continue to lag in telehealth adoption despite industry investment. Finally, the public health landscape includes a concerning Ebola outbreak in eastern Congo with over 1,700 deaths, while startups like Apnimed are pursuing novel delivery mechanisms for sleep apnea treatment and function health secured $450 million in growth financing, showing continued venture interest despite regulatory uncertainty.",
